[Music]  think different we have all seen these  apples Apple commercials get creative  innovate our society encourages new  ideas and perspectives yet often  stigmatizes people who neurologically  think differently how many of you have  or know someone with dyslexia  how about ADHD what about someone on the  autism spectrum yeah we all do one and  58 people have autism one in 1080hd  another one in 10 have dyslexia and we  have always been there logically  different yet our education systems our  schools our social norms are all  designed for the majority or  neurotypical mind we seek to think  differently yet we exclude different  thinkers this is my little brother  Steven he's 24 he is a painter a  philosopher a mental health advocate and  also an awesome little brother he has  autism for those of you who aren't  familiar the autism spectrum is a  continuum of traits that impacts how a  person perceives or socializes with  others this difference in brain  development can cause mild to severe  impairments it is a wide spectrum and it  affects people no two people the same  way where's Steven he is able to  communicate and he understands that he  has autism that he thinks differently  yet sometimes his autism can make it  really difficult to connect with others  but I am excited for this time period in  history diversity and inclusion are  being sought after and celebrated brave  change makers have fought the in  justices of other and we're finally  seeing and appreciating the value of our  differences there is a long way to go  across previous divides around race  gender and sexual orientation yet I  don't see inclusion as zero-sum and I'd  love to add to this rich conversation  the concept of neuro diversity neuro  diversity is a term first coined in 1988  by Judy singer the Australian  sociologist and it is a concept that  regards individuals with differences in  brain function and behavioral traits as  part of the normal variation in the  human population because like bio  diversity that accounts for the rich  vibrance in our natural environment  neurodiversity accounts for the fact  that all of our brains are  neurologically distinct whether it is  autism ADHD or another neuro divergency  we really all do think differently I  fell in love with this term because it  doesn't focus first on impairment  my hope is that through the lens of  neurodiversity we can celebrate each  other's differences and see the person  first and not the disability or  different ability my hope is to increase  your awareness your understanding and  encourage inclusion to build narrow  diverse communities because you can't  have inclusion without increasing your  understanding and this understanding  requires a broader awareness without  these three elements if you interact  with a neurologically different person  you may be thinking to yourselves things  like what a weirdo  what a pity this isn't my problem these  thoughts I'd love to change instead into  a conversation where we build  relationship I'd like to take you  through my own journey and all three of  these stuff let's start with awareness  as I shared I grew up in a neurodiverse  home through conversations with my  brother I learned a lot around how  autism affects him and also through  seeing how other people treat him when  we're in public if Steven is feeling  overwhelmed in his environment because  of the sensory changes with autism to  cope or soothe he'll paced back and  forth he may seem as arms or scratches  head repeatedly and I watched how others  respond to this behavior I've seen even  neighbors move away maybe to the other  side of the street even shield their  children from my brother and I wonder if  they think what bullies have called him  his whole life  or said to him  what a weirdo yet I believe all these  people to be good people and that if  they understood he was different or how  he was different if they were aware of  the differences of neurodiversity  and autism then instead they would start  a conversation they would say hello how  are you  the next step is understanding I spent  my summer living in intentionally  inclusive community where neuro-diverse  people live together and shared housing  and life and I found even myself  thinking this what a pity what a pity  that my new roommates have challenges  around things that I take for granted  every day and when it comes to different  abilities this can be the default but it  doesn't have to be our default my  understanding increased as I built real  relationships with people different from  myself I met Nadia and we went dancing  all night long and Marie whose love of  music took us to the local music school  for lessons and my neurodivergent friend  Carrie who despite being only able to  communicate through eye blinks got her  best friend laughing up a storm every  single day I learned through my  understanding that I was wrong to start  from a place of pity and instead I want  to start from a place of curiosity  expecting connection the late ability  and friendship I started saying who are  you instead the next step is inclusion  I'd like you to meet Cole Cole is the  president of spectrum autism at the  University of Berkeley  and when he joined the autism Club he  found himself to be the only autistic  person in it think for a moment if our  women and management group here were led  by a man or if there were no women in it  yeah Cole shared with me his story and  his journey to college and how at times  he would hide his autism to avoid stigma  and how difficult this made building  genuine community so when he arrived at  Cal and met students interested in  building narrow diverse communities  he was embraced and now he's leading the  charge for inclusion there he says I am  the first president of spectrum autism  at Cal but I will not be the last with  all the challenges we have in the world  today it's not uncommon to think this  that this is not my problem this is not  my issue this is not my community  whether it is in our schools in our work  certainly in our neighborhoods  there are neurodivergent people  everywhere and oftentimes especially  with the challenges of connecting with  autism there's a lot of isolation how  can we take one step closer and make it  a little easier to connect how can we  change this is not my problem - this is  my opportunity and we are in this  together hopefully I have increased your  awareness increased your understanding  and encouraged you to think a little  differently about inclusion how do you  start just say hello how are you who are  you we are in this together  you  [Applause]  [Music]

